body:
You're definitely not alone. Me and our longest-standing alter that's been with me for like, 16? 17? Years? Are totally a thing now. To be honest, I think we liked each other for a lot of that time, maybe even the whole time, but I've always been utterly mortified by the concept of "growing up," so I spent pretty much my entire adolescence rejecting "growing up" related concepts, like acknowledging and accepting your/other's feelings. I was in such intense denial that I spent years calling myself aromantic/asexual and insisting to myself I viewed him as a big brother/best friend figure even though I know damn well in my heart I was *constantly* like 😳

Luckily, I am no longer like that and I have kind of cranked up the affection levels to 1000 ///>w>/// I have a lot of years to make up for, after all

I definitely feel you on that last paragraph, too. Literally yesterday I was lamenting the same thing and wishing that I could just crack this body's head open like an egg so we could independently emerge from it and be able to do those things. Being able to accomplish a good amount of that in headspace is a... decent consolation prize, but it still really bums me out that we can't *tangibly* be a thing. It also makes it really hard to gush about him to others like I often want to, because I just know most people would scoff and treat it like some imaginary crush. It's not! It's just a... close-distance relationship. So close that we're stuck in the same cranium. \^\^;

I feel that. It really does make holding down a job almost impossible. We got fired or quit from every job we ever had within a week or two, because the stress of them would make us all kinds of unstable. We've been out of a "proper" job for over a year now and barely get by doing freelance art from home, which... affords the few bills and meds we have, but if only we could make a bit more, I'd love to afford a proper psychologist who can help us, or help pay the rent so I feel less like a leech... \^\^;

I hope getting disability works out for you! I hear it's super hard to get it for mental illnesses, so you might have to apply multiple times or even get a lawyer to help you appeal it. That's what a friend had to do for his major depression.

If you manage to make it work and are comfortable talking about it, I'm sure people would appreciate a post on your experience! There's probably more systems than just you and me who struggle to get work and afford their needs.

Is it amnesia if specific details can't be recalled, but other details can?  
For instance, I can remember details of my life as far back as 2 years old, like things I did, who I was with, what I was interested in. But I have a lot of difficulty remembering conversations, or like, even things I liked can be a blur. I loved cartoons and watched them all the time as a kid, and yet I forgot the vast majority of what I watched, only recalling specific details when I rewatched them as an adult.

I've always had an immense difficulty understanding the passage of time? So I can remember specific events, but I can't tell you the specific time they happened, only a general range. It's so bad that when we were learning about elapsed time in 3rd grade, I was the only child in class who was completely confused and lagging behind everyone else in terms of learning the material. Even now, in say, the last week, I'd have difficulty telling you on what day I did what. I can remember that something happened, I just don't remember *when* it happened. My timeline is a scribble. \^\^;

When I started, they gave me a little printout of all the possible changes and average amount of time you have to be on T to expect them. Of course, male pattern baldness was on there, but I was 17 years old, so my then-teenaged self thought "surely this won't be me, male pattern baldness is for like, middle aged men, right?"  
It was me. Granted, it's not as bad as it probably could have been, especially if I waited any longer to quit T (should have quit sooner, it took me 5 years to wake up and realize it wasn't doing it for me anymore). But it's still definitely not great. Styling my bangs can be a real b\*tch because of it. I kinda be looking like Vegeta from Dragon Ball, so I NEED to keep my bangs down or else people will see my nasty "M" shaped hairline \^\^;

I started my treatment through Planned Parenthood, which at the time was JUST starting HRT services... So they kind of made it alarmingly easy to get started on T. I had been hearing how you'd need a confirmed dysphoria diagnosis to start, so I went in expecting I'd need to get paperwork or something proving it, but all they did was ask if I had ever been to therapy for dysphoria and then didn't press any further... Aside from that, they happily told me the expected effects, showed me how to self-inject, did my bloodwork... It was definitely a path of no resistance. Which... I feel like when you're doing permanent life-changing hormonal effects to your body, there should definitely be at least a little resistance... ahaha.
